Welcome to Starfall!

You have been presented with an honorary copy of STARFALL version 1.0, by
Mark Porter.  All of the documentation and artwork is courtesy of Jeanne
Barnett and Susan Gager.  Much thanks is given to John Stevens for overall
guidance and design overview.  In addition, Professor Charles Howerton
deserves praise for allowing the development of such a project in a junior-
level computer science.

For an explanation of the game, you can read the introduction in the
manual (enclosed).

In order to play the game, the following steps must be taken:

1. Follow the installation procedures given in Appendix A of
   the manual, EXCEPT FOR THE FOLLOWING:

   *** DO NOT LOAD THE OVERLAY FILES (PLAYGAME.0??) ***
   *** ONTO THE RAMDISK - STARFALL NOW DOES THIS    ***
   *** TOTALLY AUTOMATICALLY.                       ***

2. Run INSTALL.COM. (Just type INSTALL at the MS-DOS prompt)

3. Configure a ramdisk (128K minimum) on your system
   with the same drive designation that you told
   INSTALL.COM.

4. Run STARFALL.COM (Just type STARFALL at the MS-DOS prompt)

Disk Contents:

  Disk 1: Contains all binary (.COM) images of the major programs. Also
          contains all overlay (.0XX) files for these programs. The overlay
          programs are necessary since the IBM can only support at most
          a 64K block of code.  The main code for STARFALL compiles to
          over 170K, so overlays are essential.  In simpler terms, these
          are the programs that are actually run.  Only one, however, is
          of interest to the user: STARFALL.COM.  All others are accessible
          from the main STARFALL menu.

  Disk 2: Contains all necessary data files for the simulation, including
          help files, matrix files, resource information files.  In simpler
          terms, this means all the information about how the worlds work
          and what items (i.e. Space shuttles, jeeps, jungles, etc) are
          allowed.

  Disk 3: Contains VOLTAR.DAT, a world that has been started, with a few
          teams of miners exploring, many transport rockets in the midst
          of transit, etc.

